## Refactoring Complete

**File:** `/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-08-11_05-07-07_game-of-life-example-mapping_v6.6-lab-split-cc_opus-5-no-thinking/src/game-of-life.ts`

### Naming evaluation (first priority)

`nextGeneration`, `neighbors`, `key`, `living`, and `candidates` all fit their purpose and were kept. One name was actively wrong:

**`survivors` held both survivors and newborn cells.** The array accumulated cells passing the birth rule (`count === 3` for dead cells) alongside genuine survivors. A reader scanning for reproduction support would conclude it was missing. This was the Rule 2 violation worth fixing, and it will only get more misleading as the four remaining reproduction/oscillator todos are activated.

### Changes applied (one at a time, tests run after each)

**1. Rule 2 — reveal intent.** Replaced the `survivors` accumulator and its unnamed ternary with a predicate that names the two Game of Life rules:

```ts
const isAliveNextGeneration = (cell: Cell): boolean => {
  const liveNeighbors = liveNeighborCount(cell);
  const survives = liveNeighbors === 2 || liveNeighbors === 3;
  const isBorn = liveNeighbors === 3;
  return living.has(key(cell)) ? survives : isBorn;
};

return [...candidates.values()].filter(isAliveNextGeneration);
```

The old `isAlive ? count === 2 || count === 3 : count === 3` encoded survival and reproduction without naming either. `survives` and `isBorn` map directly onto the spec's rule names and onto the pending todo tests.

**2. Rule 3 — remove duplication.** The candidate loop called `candidates.set(key(c), c)` twice, once for the cell and once per neighbor. A cell and its neighborhood are one concept, so they now flow through a single insertion point:

```ts
for (const candidate of [cell, ...neighbors(cell)]) {
  candidates.set(key(candidate), candidate);
}
```

### APP mass

Roughly 148 → 139 (Δ −9). The named `survives`/`isBorn` bindings add mass (+2) while removing a raw conditional and the push-mutation accumulator (−6 assignment, −4 conditional); the dedup trades a `set` invocation for a spread. Note that the mass reduction is incidental here — the primary justification is Rule 2, which outranks APP. I would have made change 1 even if mass had gone up, because `survivors` misnames what the code does.

### Verification

`pnpm test` green after each change individually (4 passed, 7 todo), and `tsc --noEmit` exits clean. Behavior is unchanged — the filter evaluates the same predicate over the same candidate set, and `Map` still dedupes by key, so output ordering is identical.
